    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§352, 353, 355 & 359, Ec Dev L",
    "lawSection": "Economic Development Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9467\n\nSPONSOR: COONEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the economic development law, in relation to allowing\nfor eligibility of Green CHIPS projects in the Excelsior tax credit\nprogram\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation amends the Excelsior Jobs Tax Credit Program to include\nGreen CHIPS projects and provides certain special provisions within the\nprogram to account for the unique nature of Green CHIPS Projects. This\nlegislation would allow NYS to access Federal CHIPS funding and attract\nnew semiconductor manufacturing and related material supplier projects\nto locate in New York.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. This section defines a Green CHIPS project as Green CHIPS\nProjects are semiconductor manufacturing and related material supplier\nprojects and provides criteria for such projects including a 500 job and\n$3 billion investment; establishes a benefit cost ratio of 15 to 1;\nstates that such projects would be able to get ten years of credits for\nthis initial investment in phase one and an additional 10 years of cred-\nit for a second phase; provides that only the first $200,000 of gross\nwages per job will be eligible for a credit; and exempts the program\nfrom the current schedule; establishes that Green CHIPS projects would\nbe eligible for the level of credits allowed for green projects; creates\na $500 million cap annually for Green CHIPS projects from tax years 2022\nthrough 2041; and provides that any unused credits may be rolled over\ninto future years, all unused credits must be issued by January 1, 2050.\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis proposal from the Executive would allow Green CHIPS projects to\napply for credits under the Excelsior Jobs Program.  The Executive has\nasked for this program to try to take advantage of the federal push to\nbring semiconductor manufacturing back into the United States which\ncould make $52 billion in federal support for semiconductor manufactur-\ning projects around the country available.  These federal monies would\nbe tied to some form of state support.  According to the Executive, they\nwant the state to be competitive in order to bring these very large\nprojects to the State. These projects stand to significantly transform\nany locality in which such a project is established, given the magnitude\nof the investments being made and the relatively high wages these high-\ntech manufacturing projects create.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTBD.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n",
